    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter June 1st with a condensed lineup, prioritizing high-efficiency matchups despite a slate of challenging opposing starters. **Christian Yelich** headlines our offensive output today with a score of 63.76; his leadoff role combined with a strong dynamic platoon advantage against Roupp (+13%) makes him our clear anchor. While **Junior Caminero** faces a tougher test against the elite 2.31 xERA of Madden, he remains a critical start (48.90) due to his high-leverage cleanup spot. **Carson Benge** also secures a spot in the UTIL slot (54.69), leveraging his leadoff positioning to mitigate the neutral matchup against Hancock. We are proceeding cautiously with **Luke Raley** and **Colt Keith**; both hover just above our 40.0 efficiency floor, as their scores are suppressed by opposing SP skills and park factors, though they remain essential for maintaining our aggregate production in Roto scoring.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the morning involved **Chase Meidroth**, whose score of 36.86 fell just short of our daily threshold. Despite a modest park factor boost, the combination of a difficult SP matchup against Ryan and a negative platoon adjustment kept him pinned to the bench. Similarly, **Casey Schmitt** was considered, but his disadvantageous platoon against the lefty Drohan rendered him a sub-floor option. We remain particularly sensitive to the scarcity of stolen bases within our current configuration, keeping eyes on potential late-inning swaps if game scripts for **Trea Turner**, **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, or **Caleb Durbin** shift, though they are not currently scheduled to anchor the daily lineup. \n\nRisk management remains a priority today, as the pitching matchups are generally unfavorable across the board. We are monitoring the status of **Yordan Alvarez** closely, as the team remains in an off-day cycle that leaves a significant void in our power categories. With **Kyle Schwarber**, **Hyeseong Kim**, **Kazuma Okamoto